{
    "title": "Strengthening the Section 184 Indian Housing Loan Guarantee Program; Extension of Compliance Date",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 29, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-29/pdf/2025-23884.pdf",
    "summary": "The latest federal rules focus on small, targeted changes across aviation, housing, and justice\u2014aimed more at housekeeping than sweeping reform.\n\nIn aviation, the Federal Aviation Administration updated flight procedures and takeoff standards for a range of airports, with changes taking effect in late January 2026. These updates are routine and meant to keep charts and procedures current. The agency says the economic impact will be minimal. In housing, the Department of Housing and Urban Development is indefinitely delaying the compliance date for its rule to strengthen the Section 184 Indian Home Loan Guarantee Program. HUD plans to finish a new handbook, consult with tribes and lenders, and provide training before setting a new date, with at least 30 to 90 days\u2019 notice once ready. In justice, the Department of Justice is folding the Office of the Executive Secretariat back into its management division, reversing a recent standalone structure to streamline internal operations, effective immediately.\n\nWho benefits? Pilots, air traffic teams, and airports gain from updated flight procedures that reflect current conditions and support safe operations. Tribal governments, lenders, servicers, and homebuyers who use the Section 184 program should benefit from clearer guidance and training before new rules are enforced, reducing confusion and the risk of uneven implementation. Within the Justice Department, consolidating offices may reduce duplication and speed up internal processes.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Homebuyers and communities relying on the Section 184 program could face continued uncertainty while the compliance date is on hold, and improvements intended to reduce risk and strengthen servicing will take longer to arrive. Organizations that prepared early for the HUD rule may need to pause or redo some work once the new handbook is issued. In aviation, while impacts are expected to be small, some operators may need to update their procedures and training to match the revised approaches. Inside the Justice Department, staff may experience short-term disruption as roles and workflows shift.\n\nOverall, these actions aim to keep systems current, provide clearer guidance before enforcement, and streamline internal operations, with limited immediate effects on the broader public.",
